1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,496
Last month I ran twelve interviews.

2
00:00:02,536 --> 00:00:04,760
Then someone asked me, how many people

3
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:05,893
actually said that?

4
00:00:07,233 --> 00:00:08,836
Here is what most of us do.

5
00:00:08,876 --> 00:00:11,331
Paste the transcripts into a chat window,

6
00:00:11,371 --> 00:00:13,156
and get back something fluent.

7
00:00:13,196 --> 00:00:15,590
But the model only sees what you pasted.

8
00:00:15,630 --> 00:00:17,902
It cannot count people, and nothing it

9
00:00:17,942 --> 00:00:19,971
says can be traced back to anyone.

10
00:00:21,111 --> 00:00:22,772
So I built Research Lens.

11
00:00:22,812 --> 00:00:25,628
An MCP server that lets an assistant query

12
00:00:25,668 --> 00:00:27,941
your research repository directly.

13
00:00:28,981 --> 00:00:29,845
Same question.

14
00:00:29,885 --> 00:00:31,266
Now it goes and looks.

15
00:00:32,206 --> 00:00:34,655
Round one, one person negative, one of

16
00:00:34,695 --> 00:00:35,637
three finished.

17
00:00:35,677 --> 00:00:37,798
Round two, two positive, everyone

18
00:00:37,838 --> 00:00:40,156
finished, satisfaction up twenty six

19
00:00:40,196 --> 00:00:40,614
points.

20
00:00:42,054 --> 00:00:44,278
And every quote comes back with who said

21
00:00:44,318 --> 00:00:46,259
it, which round, and the timestamp.

22
00:00:47,499 --> 00:00:49,640
Then it does the thing I care about most.

23
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:51,449
It tells you when not to trust it.

24
00:00:53,089 --> 00:00:55,822
Free, open source, and on npm today.
